t{5} + \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{5} - \\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{7})(\\sqrt{5} + \\sqrt{6} - \\sqrt{7}),\\] we can use the method of polynomial factorization and symmetry.\n\nFirst, let's denote:\n\\[a = \\sqrt{5}, \\quad b = \\sqrt{6}, \\quad c = \\sqrt{7}.\\]\nThe product then becomes:\n\\[(a + b + c)(-a + b + c)(a - b + c)(a + b - c).\\]\n\nWe can use the identity for the product of four terms of this form. Consider the polynomial \\(P(x) = (x + a + b + c)(x - a + b + c)(x + a - b + c)(x - a - b + c)\\). When \\(x = 0\\), this polynomial evaluates to the product we are looking for. Notice that:\n\\[P(x) = \\left((x + b + c) + a\\right)\\left((x + b + c) - a\\right)\\left((x - b + c) + a\\right)\\left((x - b + c) - a\\right).\\]\nThis can be simplified using the difference of squares:\n\\[P(x) = \\left((x + b + c)^2 - a^2\\right)\\left((x - b + c)^2 - a^2\\right).\\]\nExpanding each term, we get:\n\\[(x + b + c)^2 - a^2 = x^2 + 2x(b + c) + (b + c)^2 - a^2,\\]\n\\[(x - b + c)^2 - a^2 = x^2 - 2x(b - c) + (b - c)^2 - a^2.\\]\nSo,\n\\[P(x) = \\left(x^2 + 2x(b + c) + (b + c)^2 - a^2\\right)\\left(x^2 - 2x(b - c) + (b - c)^2 - a^2\\right).\\]\nWhen \\(x = 0\\), we have:\n\\[P(0) = \\left((b + c)^2 - a^2\\right)\\left((b - c)^2 - a^2\\right).\\]\nNow, we calculate each term:\n\\[(b + c)^2 - a^2 = 6 + 7 + 2\\sqrt{42} - 5 = 8 + 2\\sqrt{42},\\]\n\\[(b - c)^2 - a^2 = 6 + 7 - 2\\sqrt{42} - 5 = 8 - 2\\sqrt{42}.\\]\nThus,\n\\[P(0) = (8 + 2\\sqrt{42})(8 - 2\\sqrt{42}) = 8^2 - (2\\sqrt{42})^2 = 64 - 4 \\cdot 42 = 64 - 168 = -104.\\]\nTherefore, the value of the original product is:\n\\[\\boxed{-104}.\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{-104}","correct":false,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:847"}
